#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["markdown-it-py>=3", "mdit-py-plugins>=0.4", "pyyaml>=6"]
# ///
"""mt - run state, journal and agterm UI for the make-task skill.

The make-task orchestrator calls mt at every step boundary. mt owns the run
directory (state.json, state.js for the dashboard, artifacts) and is the
only thing that talks to agtermctl. Every command loads the state fresh, changes
it and saves it atomically right away.

Usage:
    mt [--run DIR] init --title T --profile-dir DIR
    mt [--run DIR] status
    mt [--run DIR] set KEY=VALUE ...
    mt [--run DIR] phase ID [--activity A] [--note N] [--skipped]
    mt [--run DIR] activity A [--wait]
    mt [--run DIR] assume TEXT --why W
    mt [--run DIR] decide Q A
    mt [--run DIR] event TEXT
    mt [--run DIR] checkpoint begin KEY [--resume]
    mt [--run DIR] checkpoint end KEY [--result R]
    mt [--run DIR] fail MSG
    mt [--run DIR] dirty [--exclude PATH ...]
    mt [--run DIR] plan PATH [--progress P]
    mt [--run DIR] refresh
    mt [--run DIR] artifact ID PATH [--hint H]
    mt [--run DIR] artifact changes [--hint H]
    mt [--run DIR] review start --round N --run NAME --round-dir D
    mt [--run DIR] review wait --round N [--deadline S]
    mt [--run DIR] review result FILE --round N [--not-gating ID=REASON ...]
                                [--deferred ID=REASON ...] [--confirming]
    mt [--run DIR] review verify-final
    mt [--run DIR] done --status S [--note N]
    mt [--run DIR] revmux aside --state-dir DIR --toplevel DIR
    mt [--run DIR] revmux put-back --state-dir DIR --toplevel DIR
    mt profile resolve|check|status|install-default [--profile NAME | --dir DIR]
    mt profile import ID --source S [--replace] [--profile NAME | --dir DIR]
    mt profile outdated [--network] [--profile NAME | --dir DIR]
    mt profile fetch ID [--profile NAME | --dir DIR]
    mt profile accept ID --expect HASH [--profile NAME | --dir DIR]

Run lookup: --run DIR, else the pointer file
<state root>/current-<AGTERM_SESSION_ID or "default">, written by init.
State root: $MT_STATE_HOME, else ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/make-task.
Config root: $MT_CONFIG_HOME, else ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/make-task.

Environment:
    MT_STATE_HOME     state root override
    MT_CONFIG_HOME    config root override (profiles live in <config root>/profiles/<name>/)
    MT_PLUGINS_JSON   installed_plugins.json, read for plugin: profile sources only
                      (default ~/.claude/plugins/installed_plugins.json)
    MT_PGREP          process matcher for `review wait` (default pgrep; called as `MT_PGREP -f -- PATTERN`)
    MT_REVIEW_POLL    seconds between `review wait` polls (default 2)
    MT_PROFILE_LOCK_WAIT
                      seconds a profile command waits for another one changing the same profile (default 60)
    AGTERM_ENABLED, AGTERM_SESSION_ID, AGTERM_PANE_ID
                      agterm is used only when the first two are set; nothing calls agtermctl otherwise

Init: `init --profile-dir DIR` runs `profile check` on DIR (without the update lookups) before
anything is written and fails with its errors, creating no run; a `template: true` profile is
refused too. It stores the profile's resolved values as flat run-state keys, printed by init and
`status`: profile_name, profile_dir, autonomy, gates (the profile's or its autonomy preset's),
defaults, brainstorm_entry, plan_entry, plan_reviewer, plan_reviewer_model (the reviewer's
frontmatter `model`), exec_root (the exec companion), exec_scripts, exec_rules, exec_data
(<profile>/data/<exec companion>), review_entry, review_scripts, review_data; paths are absolute
and a skipped phase's keys are null. default_branch is the last line <exec_scripts>/detect-branch.sh
prints within 60 s (else local git steps). The check warnings (a review `requires` command missing
on PATH among them) and one for a review phase that is `skip` join init's warnings. `status` warns
on a run whose state has none of these keys (started before profiles).

Dashboard: init copies assets/dashboard.html into the run dir. In agterm it shows the
split when none is shown (created_split records a split mt made), opens the page with
`--cwd <run> --js --chromeless` over the pane the agent does not run in, and hands the
focus back. Any failing agtermctl call there leaves `agterm: false` and init goes on.
`phase` and `activity` (not `--wait`) re-assert the sidebar status `active`, `fail`
sets `blocked`; these are best-effort and only warn on failure.

Artifacts render to <run>/artifacts/<id>.html, again only when the source mtime
changed (on `artifact`, `plan` and `refresh`): mt renders the markdown itself
(markdown-it, raw HTML off, with tables, strikethrough, task lists, footnotes and
`doc-` heading ids) into assets/doc.html; a source that is not markdown renders as
a code block. Rendering is best-effort: a source mt cannot read prints a warning
and keeps the previous html. design, stats, plan, progress and review also get
<run>/artifacts/<id>.js, `window.mtArtifact("<id>", {...})` in ASCII-escaped JSON,
rewritten with a new `updated` only when the rest of it changed: design and stats hold
{title, html, toc, source, updated} (title: the first `#` heading, else the artifact's
label), plan adds each task's checkbox `items`, progress holds {source, header, entries,
raw, updated}, the log parsed into entries (parse_progress) and its whole text, review
holds {rounds, updated} (review_payload), written by `review start`, `wait` and `result`.

Review rounds: `review start` (note: its --run is the revmux run name; the global
--run DIR goes before the command) records the round's `head` (HEAD of cwd's toplevel: what the
round reviews) and prints the round's report path,
<run>/revmux/round-<n>.json, where the launch writes revmux's stdout, and registers the
review artifact (source <run>/review.md). `review wait` prints `done`, `failed` or
`running` (deadline reached); on each poll where the round's events.jsonl changed (size or
mtime) it rewrites artifacts/review.js (events.jsonl folded, read whole up to 8 MB) and
saves the round's summary `live` when it changed. `review result` records a round (its
`report` becomes the file it read); state `unresolved` and `not_gating` are the latest
round's, `deferred` collects every round's deferred findings since the last `--confirming`
round (the leftovers fix round settles them). `review verify-final` prints `unchanged` or `changed`, sets
review_stale and stamps verified_sha: HEAD's tree must equal reviewed_sha's tree
replayed onto HEAD's merge-base with origin/<default> (or <default>); a conflicting
replay is `changed`, and with no rebase target the trees are compared as they are.
Uncommitted changes outside `.revmux/` are `changed` too, and so are changes to the
stash_paths until `stash_restored` records that the restore put them back.

Dirty paths: `dirty` re-reads `git status` (every untracked file listed on its own),
drops `.revmux/` and the --exclude paths, stores the rest as stash_paths and prints
{"paths": [...], "quoted": "<shell-quoted paths>", "unstage": [...], "unstage_quoted": "..."}
for the in-place stash; `unstage` are the paths in HEAD the index no longer has (staged
deletions, rename sources), the only ones the stash recipe unstages.

Completion: `done --status S` (clean, "not clean", "review skipped", "review stale",
stopped) stamps the run and its running phase finished (an `error` phase keeps its
status), sets now_kind done and the sidebar status completed. `clean` is refused while
findings are unresolved or deferred, the review is stale, skipped, never ran, has no
recorded round, its latest round has no result or was degraded, reviewed_sha is not the
`head` of the latest finished round (commits after it were never reviewed), verify-final
has not checked reviewed_sha, the finalize checkpoint did not end `ok`, or an error is set.
`set reviewed_sha` is write-once: a second, different value is refused.
A second call keeps the first finish time.

.revmux/ working copies (in place, Finalize and Completion): `revmux aside` saves every
tracked .revmux/ path whose working copy differs, in bytes, type or executable bit, from
what `git checkout-index` writes: its `ls-files -s -z` record goes to
<state-dir>/revmux-tree/list, a raw copy (a symlink as a symlink) to revmux-tree/files/,
made in revmux-tree.new/ and renamed once every copy compares equal; then it removes the
listed paths and checks .revmux out from the index. A rerun keeps the first save: before
anything is removed, every saved record must still be in the index whole and its path hold
the index version, the saved copy or nothing, and every path edited now must be saved;
anything else is differs.
`revmux put-back` copies the saved files back, all or nothing, once every listed record
is still in the index whole and each path holds HEAD's version, the saved copy or
nothing; then renames revmux-tree/ to revmux-tree.restored/. Paths, records and symlink
targets are handled as bytes. A symlink or other non-directory in a parent component of
a path under the toplevel or under revmux-tree/ is refused before anything is touched:
aside prints unsafe, put-back conflict. Both print one line revmux_tree=<outcome>: aside prints
aside or none (exit 0), differs, unsafe or failed (exit 1); put-back prints ok, none,
conflict or failed, always exit 0. Neither reads the run state.

Profiles: `profile` commands take --profile NAME (default `default`, under
<config root>/profiles/) or --dir DIR (any profile dir, e.g. the template), never --run, and
print one JSON line. `resolve` prints {"name", "dir", "status": "ok"|"missing"} (ok: profile.md
exists). `check` validates profile.md (schema, phases, skip rules, entries, the exec and review
companion contracts, the plan reviewer's frontmatter, autonomy, gates, defaults, tokens left in
the copies and profile.md, and UPDATE.md's `rewritten_for`) and prints {"ok", "errors",
"warnings", "updates"}, exit 1 when not ok. A token no run fills in is an error: a path variable
the install did not rewrite (${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}, ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}, ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA},
their $VAR forms, a ${VAR:-x}-style form) or ${user_config.…}; a `template: true` profile is
scanned as an install would leave it. Any other $CLAUDE_… variable is a warning. `rewritten_for`
(the dir install-default, import and accept rewrote the copies for) other than the profile's dir
is an error: the profile was copied or moved. A review `requires` command missing on PATH is a
warning (`requires` is a review field only); `updates` are outdated's for the make-task: and
plugin: sources (one it cannot read is a warning). `status` prints autonomy, gates (resolved),
defaults, phases and every companion with its source, version and `modified` (companions/<id>
differs from upstream/<id>; null without an upstream/<id>).
`install-default` installs the skill's template assets/profiles/default (profile.md must say
`template: true`) as the profile (or into --dir DIR), which must not exist: companions/<id> and
upstream/<id> both from the template's companions/<id>, rewritten as `import` does (the template's
own upstream/ and UPDATE.md are not copied); UPDATE.md records the target as `rewritten_for` and
each companion as make-task:default/<id> with its sha256: tree hash under empty ## Preferences,
## Changes and ## Wanted; profile.md is the template's with `template: false`; LICENSES/ is
copied; data/<id>/ starts empty. It is built in a temp dir next to the target and renamed into
place, so a failure leaves no profile behind. It prints {"name", "dir", "template", "companions":
{<id>: {"source", "version"}}}.
`import ID --source S` copies a source to companions/ID and upstream/ID and records
{source, version} in UPDATE.md's frontmatter (the file is created when missing; an UPDATE.md it
cannot read fails the import before anything is copied; `rewritten_for` is set when a rewritten
import finds none); an existing companion needs --replace. A subpath that resolves outside its
source (a symlink) fails. Sources and their version: make-task:<profile>/<id> (the skill's
assets/profiles/<profile>/companions/<id>; sha256: of that tree), plugin:<name>@<marketplace>
[:<subpath>] (installPath from MT_PLUGINS_JSON; <version>+<gitCommitSha[:12]>),
git:<url>#<ref>[:<subpath>] (fetched at depth 1 into a temp dir, the subpath is the plugin root;
the commit sha; `#` and the last `:` after it split the spec), local (companions/ID written by
the user; version null, no upstream/ID). Left out of a copy: .git and .DS_Store at any depth,
.in_use/ and hooks/ at its root. A symlink is copied as what it points to; one that leaves the
source, dangles or loops fails the import. Unless profile.md says `template: true`, every UTF-8
text file gets ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT} -> <profile>/companions/ID, ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR} -> the
nearest ancestor dir holding a SKILL.md (else the companion root), ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA} ->
<profile>/data/ID (braced, or unbraced up to a word boundary), always for the final companions/ID
location; other files are copied byte for byte, modes kept. ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT} in a plugin
subtree fails: the plugin root is above the copy.
`outdated` compares each recorded version with the source's version now and prints
{"updates": [{"id", "source", "version", "available", "modified"}], "current": [ids],
"skipped": [{"id", "source", "reason"}], "errors": [{"id", "source", "error"}]} (exit 0); git:
sources are looked up (a shallow fetch of the ref, the commit import would record) only with
--network; local ones and copies with no recorded source are skipped. `fetch ID` copies the
source as it is now to .staging/ID/tree, rewritten for companions/ID like `import` (its source
and version in .staging/ID/fetch.json; an earlier fetch is replaced) and prints {"id", "source",
"version", "available", "staging", "tree", "changed", "modified", "diff"}, staging being the
staged tree, tree its sha256: hash and diff the unified diff upstream/ID -> .staging/ID/tree.
A fetch is made whole in a private dir and renamed into place, so what it prints is its own.
`accept ID --expect HASH` claims .staging/ID with one rename, moves its tree to upstream/ID,
and copies it to companions/ID when that copy is unmodified (or missing), records the fetched
version, removes the staging and prints {"id", "source", "version", "previous",
"copy": "updated"|"kept", "modified"}; it refuses without a fetch, when the claimed tree is not
HASH (the `tree` of the fetch that was reviewed: a later fetch, from another session too,
replaced it; the staging is put back), or when UPDATE.md records another source since. An
`import` drops a pending fetch of that companion.
`raise-schema` sets profile.md's `schema` line (nothing else in the file changes) to the schema
this make-task reads, once every make-task: companion records its template's current version, and
prints {"dir", "schema", "previous", "raised"} (raised false: it was current already). It refuses
a template, a newer schema, and, naming them, make-task: companions not at that version yet:
an older schema fails `check`, and `/make-task --update` takes those updates, then runs this.
install-default, import, fetch (once its copy is made), accept and raise-schema hold the
profile's lock, an flock on its dir (install-default: on the dir it installs into), for the whole
change, so two sessions never interleave them; one that finds the lock held waits up to MT_PROFILE_LOCK_WAIT
seconds, then fails with `another make-task command is changing profile <name>` (install-default:
`... the profiles in <dir>`), changing nothing. resolve, check, status, outdated and init take no lock.
A profile's path must be one plain shell word, with no whitespace and none of
' " ` $ & | ; < > ( ) [ ] { } * ? ! # ~ \\ ^: the rewritten paths and SKILL.md's script calls
use it unquoted, so install-default, a rewriting import or fetch refuse any other target, and
`check` (so `init` too) reports it as an error. So must every part of those paths: `check`
reports a phases path that is not one, and a rewrite refuses a ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR} whose skill
dir is not.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 failure (message on stderr).
"""

